1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,502
Chai Corner has six people now.

2
00:00:02,802 --> 00:00:06,122
But when Ravi steps out, everyone
does things their own way.

3
00:00:06,672 --> 00:00:09,220
One helper gives free chai to his friends.

4
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:11,813
Another forgets to write down the sales.

5
00:00:12,113 --> 00:00:15,376
And the best shift goes to a
friend, not the best worker.

6
00:00:16,026 --> 00:00:18,350
To grow, Ravi cannot be everywhere.

7
00:00:18,350 --> 00:00:19,757
He needs a rulebook.

8
00:00:20,457 --> 00:00:27,731
Meet Max Weber, a German thinker
who lived from 1864 to 1920.

9
00:00:28,031 --> 00:00:29,562
He was a sociologist.

10
00:00:29,562 --> 00:00:33,301
That means he studied how
people live and work in groups.

11
00:00:33,651 --> 00:00:37,582
His big book, Economy and
Society, came out after he died.

12
00:00:38,233 --> 00:00:42,790
In Weber's Germany, big offices ran
on files, stamps and rule books.

13
00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:48,068
Clerks did one job each, followed written
rules, and kept a record of everything.

14
00:00:48,418 --> 00:00:52,930
Weber saw that such an office runs
the same way, whoever is in charge.

15
00:00:53,580 --> 00:00:55,312
He called this bureaucracy.

16
00:00:55,612 --> 00:01:00,044
It means running an organisation by
clear written rules, with a clear chain

17
00:01:00,044 --> 00:01:00,944
of command.

18
00:01:01,266 --> 00:01:03,255
Weber called it an ideal type.

19
00:01:03,255 --> 00:01:05,490
A pure model, not a perfect one.

20
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:07,403
Let's see it work.

21
00:01:07,403 --> 00:01:09,335
Meena wants next Saturday off.

22
00:01:09,685 --> 00:01:11,868
First, she fills in a leave form.

23
00:01:11,868 --> 00:01:13,771
Nothing is just said out loud.

24
00:01:14,121 --> 00:01:17,585
The form goes up one step,
to Asha, who leads the shift.

25
00:01:17,935 --> 00:01:19,654
Asha checks the rulebook.

26
00:01:19,654 --> 00:01:22,193
Rule six says, give two days' notice.

27
00:01:22,193 --> 00:01:23,093
Meena did.

28
00:01:23,403 --> 00:01:25,656
Then it goes up to Ravi, who signs it.

29
00:01:26,006 --> 00:01:28,426
The answer comes back down the same chain.

30
00:01:28,776 --> 00:01:31,116
And Kabir writes it in the record book.

31
00:01:31,116 --> 00:01:33,533
Slower, but fair and clear for everyone.

32
00:01:34,183 --> 00:01:36,742
But why do people follow rules at all?

33
00:01:36,742 --> 00:01:39,136
Weber found three kinds of authority.

34
00:01:39,486 --> 00:01:40,386
Traditional.

35
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:43,437
We obey because it has
always been done this way.

36
00:01:43,787 --> 00:01:44,712
Charismatic.

37
00:01:44,712 --> 00:01:47,487
We obey a leader because we admire them.

38
00:01:47,837 --> 00:01:49,203
And rational legal.

39
00:01:49,203 --> 00:01:52,468
We obey the rules and
the role, not the person.

40
00:01:52,468 --> 00:01:54,704
Bureaucracy is built on this one.

41
00:01:55,354 --> 00:01:58,690
So what do Weber's key features
look like at work today?

42
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:00,468
Division of labour.

43
00:02:00,468 --> 00:02:02,651
Each person has one clear job.

44
00:02:02,651 --> 00:02:06,894
In a bank, one person opens
accounts, another approves loans.

45
00:02:07,244 --> 00:02:08,169
Hierarchy.

46
00:02:08,169 --> 00:02:09,813
A ladder of bosses.

47
00:02:09,813 --> 00:02:13,043
An army runs from general down to soldier.

48
00:02:13,393 --> 00:02:14,499
Written rules.

49
00:02:14,499 --> 00:02:16,412
The same steps every time.

50
00:02:16,412 --> 00:02:20,547
A passport office follows the
same steps for every application.

51
00:02:20,897 --> 00:02:21,944
Impersonality.

52
00:02:21,944 --> 00:02:24,172
Rules apply equally to everyone.

53
00:02:24,172 --> 00:02:27,376
A traffic fine is the
same, whoever you are.

54
00:02:27,726 --> 00:02:28,626
Merit.

55
00:02:28,376 --> 00:02:31,606
Hire and promote for
skill, not family ties.

56
00:02:31,606 --> 00:02:35,102
Many government jobs are
filled through open exams.

57
00:02:35,452 --> 00:02:36,352
Records.

58
00:02:36,188 --> 00:02:37,774
Write everything down.

59
00:02:37,774 --> 00:02:40,708
A hospital keeps a file for every patient.

60
00:02:41,358 --> 00:02:43,194
So, is bureaucracy good?

61
00:02:43,194 --> 00:02:44,937
It depends on how much.

62
00:02:45,287 --> 00:02:46,375
The good side.

63
00:02:46,375 --> 00:02:48,792
It is fair, orderly and predictable.

64
00:02:49,142 --> 00:02:50,276
The hard side.

65
00:02:50,276 --> 00:02:53,165
Too much paperwork
makes it rigid and slow.

66
00:02:53,165 --> 00:02:55,022
People call this red tape.

67
00:02:55,372 --> 00:02:59,753
The name comes from the red ribbon
once used to tie up official papers.

68
00:03:00,103 --> 00:03:04,487
A customer asks for less sugar, and a
strict helper says, not in the rules.

69
00:03:04,837 --> 00:03:09,262
Weber himself warned that too many
rules can trap people, like an iron

70
00:03:09,262 --> 00:03:10,162
cage.

71
00:03:10,171 --> 00:03:13,990
So the lesson is: use rules to
be fair, not to stop thinking.

72
00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:15,593
Quick check.

73
00:03:15,593 --> 00:03:19,274
Kabir's friend pays the same
price as every other customer.

74
00:03:19,274 --> 00:03:20,836
Which feature is this?

75
00:03:22,736 --> 00:03:23,750
Impersonality!

76
00:03:23,750 --> 00:03:27,357
The rules apply equally to
everyone, whoever they are.

77
00:03:27,957 --> 00:03:28,970
So, remember.

78
00:03:29,270 --> 00:03:33,462
Bureaucracy means clear written
rules and a clear chain of command.

79
00:03:33,812 --> 00:03:36,191
It rests on rational legal authority.

80
00:03:36,191 --> 00:03:38,580
We obey the rules, not the person.

81
00:03:38,930 --> 00:03:40,717
It brings fairness and order.

82
00:03:40,717 --> 00:03:42,602
But too much becomes red tape.

83
00:03:43,202 --> 00:03:47,630
Next time, we'll meet Elton Mayo, and
see why people work better when they

84
00:03:47,630 --> 00:03:48,536
feel valued.
